From: Gran Turismo 6 for Mac <https://github.com/Wooloomooloo2/Gran-Turismo-6-Mac>
Subject: [PATCH] SPU: a one-way stack reset clears the return point; interpreted returns match the call's frame; page-fault review fixes

Upstream: not yet submitted; written to be upstreamed (generic, no title names)
Licence: MIT, as ps3recomp (see its LICENSE); offered under the same terms
Base: ps3recomp 4e8f3ca87a3e1d87bb83b8b7e9348fe2685cd514 (after 0001-0042)

What:
- spu_run_with_halt's one-way stack reset (halt env case 2, reached through
  spu_restart_dispatch when a return lands where no drain waits) now clears
  drain_ret_pc, drain_ret_sp and irq_frame: no host frame survives the reset,
  so no return point does. Before, the innermost drain's return point stayed
  set; when the guest later returned to exactly that address at depth 0, the
  interpreter stopped on its first instruction, no trampoline was queued, and
  the run ended there (a SPURS policy module's run ended mid-job, stranding
  the jobs it had claimed).
- spu_indirect_call records the call's $sp (drain_ret_sp); the interpreter
  stops at the return point only when $sp is at or above it (the call's own
  frame or an outer one), so a recursive call through the same site, which
  returns to the same address from a deeper frame, runs on.
  spu_interp_run_until_frame is the new entry; spu_interp_run_until keeps
  its behaviour (any frame). Direct calls' drains do not know the frame and
  match any (SPU_RET_SP_ANY).
- sys_mmapper_pf: the slot state word (claim count above the state byte) is
  unsigned, so the count wraps without signed overflow. The test-only 57 hook
  gets the slot index, with an accessor for its owner (PS3_PF_TEST only).
Tests: runtime/spu/tests/test_spu_interpreter_handoff.c, new modes
nested-return-recursive (fails on the pc-only match) and
nested-indirect-restores-outer (fails if spu_indirect_call does not restore
the outer return point); tests/test_mmapper_page_fault.c checks that the
ABA test's second claimant holds the scanned slot, and that each forked
child stopped on the expected diagnostic, not on any abort.
diff --git a/runtime/spu/spu_context.h b/runtime/spu/spu_context.h
--- a/runtime/spu/spu_context.h
+++ b/runtime/spu/spu_context.h
@@ -413,6 +413,13 @@ typedef struct spu_context {
      * manager then ran its buffer loop a second time on the job's registers
      * and died in its own assert (the song-freeze). */
     uint32_t drain_ret_pc;
+    /* $sp (preferred word) of the call drain_ret_pc returns to, or
+     * SPU_RET_SP_ANY when the call's frame is not known (a direct call's
+     * drain). The interpreter stops at drain_ret_pc only in that frame or an
+     * outer one: a recursive call through the same site returns to the same
+     * address from a deeper frame (lower $sp), and must run on (batch 10
+     * review M-1). */
+    uint32_t drain_ret_sp;
 
     /* Set by another host thread to stop this SPU from outside: a raw SPU's
      * PPU writing SPU_RunCntl = 0 (spu_raw.c). Lifted code never looks at it;
@@ -480,6 +487,17 @@ static inline void spu_context_init(spu_context* ctx, uint32_t spu_id)
     ctx->status = SPU_STATUS_STOPPED;
 }
 
+/* drain_ret_sp when the call's frame is unknown: every $sp is at or above it. */
+#define SPU_RET_SP_ANY 0u
+
+/* Whether the interpreter, at drain_ret_pc, is back in the frame of the call
+ * that set it (or an outer one). The stack grows down, and a callee returns
+ * with the $sp it was called with. */
+static inline int spu_ret_frame_reached(const spu_context* ctx, uint32_t ret_sp)
+{
+    return (ctx->gpr[1]._u32[0] & SPU_LS_MASK) >= ret_sp;
+}
+
 /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * Local store access helpers
  * -----------------------------------------------------------------------*/
